{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1918 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "4ef5c09eb2906519d93bb8afe7d9fd0c33ae8cf885592a3ac1c3075922299c97" :edb/db-before-t 1917 :edb/db-after-t 1918 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535230 50 #inst "2026-10-08T20:03:18.013Z" 13194139535230 true] [17592186179454 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060128/assets/001487e4a94988a24d15702a18692e9deb2495a7cc7ec5c9f71b98fea13e0ce8.png)\n\nThe path $C_1$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(-2,0)$ to the point $O(0,0),$ and the path $C_2$ consists of a line segment from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $B(1,1).$\n\nGiven that $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = -4,$ evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r},$ where $\\mathbf{F}(x, y) = (2x-3y) \\,\\mathbf{i} + (2y-5x)\\,\\mathbf {j}$ and $C = C_1\\cup C_2.$" 13194139535230 true] [17592186179454 1100 "The path $C_1$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(-2,0)$ to the point $O(0,0)$, and the path $C_2$ consists of a line segment from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $B(1,1)$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3704/Source/Images/962f5b7322eaec11b61a09faa7e19175.png)\n\nGiven that $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = -4$, evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r}$, where $\\mathbf{F}(x, y) = (2x-3y) \\,\\mathbf{i} + (2y-5x)\\,\\mathbf {j}$ and $C = C_1\\cup C_2$." 13194139535230 false] [17592186179454 1145 "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r}\n+\n \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r}.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the first integral, so we only need to work out the second.\n\nThe path $C_2$ is the line segment from $O$ to $B.$ It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{2}(t) & =b+t(c-b) \\\\  & =⟨0,0⟩+t(⟨1,1⟩-⟨0,0⟩) \\\\  & =⟨t,t⟩ \\\\  & =t\\,i+t\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0 \\lt t \\lt 1.$ Along this segment, we have $x = t$ and $y = t.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{2}(t)) & =(2x-3y)\\,i+(2y-5x)\\,j \\\\  & =(2t-3t)\\,i+(2t-5t)\\,j \\\\  & =-t\\,i-3t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{2}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{2}(t))\\cdot {r}_{2}^{′}(t) & =(-t\\,i-3t\\,j)\\cdot (i+j) \\\\  & =-t\\cdot 1+(-3t)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-4t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =-4+{\\int }_{0}^{1}F({r}_{2}(t))\\cdot {r}_{2}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4+{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-4t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4+[-2{t}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-4+[-2+0] \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535230 true] [17592186179454 1145 "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} + \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r}\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the first integral, so we only need to work out the second.\n\nThe path $C_2$ is the line segment from $O$ to $B$. It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_{2}(t) &= b + t(c - b) \\\\\n&= ⟨0, 0⟩ + t(⟨1, 1⟩ - ⟨0, 0⟩) \\\\\n&= ⟨t, t⟩ \\\\\n&= ti + tj\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0 \\lt t \\lt 1$. Along this segment, we have $x = t$ and $y = t$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(r_{2}(t)) &= (2x - 3y)i + (2y - 5x)j \\\\\n&= (2t - 3t)i + (2t - 5t)j \\\\\n&=-ti - 3tj\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_{2}^{′}(t) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(t)j \\\\\n&= i + j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(r_{2}(t)) \\cdot r_{2}^{′}(t) &= (-ti - 3tj) \\cdot (i + j) \\\\\n&=-t \\cdot 1 + (-3t) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&=-4t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)F \\cdot dr &= ∫_(C_{1})F \\cdot dr + ∫_(C_{2})F \\cdot dr \\\\\n&=-4 + ∫_{0}^{1}F(r_{2}(t)) \\cdot r_{2}^{′}(t)dt \\\\\n&=-4 + ∫_{0}^{1}(-4t)dt \\\\\n&=-4 + [- 2t^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&=-4 + [- 2 + 0] \\\\\n&=-6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535230 false] [17592186179457 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060128/assets/38ab4af726729b5c9804f4aa13801d637e5e62eda085d6611174586827ef8c11.png)\n\nConsider the integral $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r},$ where $C$ is the closed polygonal path $FGHF$ shown above, traversed in the **clockwise** direction. Express this integral as a sum of line integrals along $C_1, C_2,$ and $C_3.$" 13194139535230 true] [17592186179457 1100 "Consider the integral $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r}$, where $C$ is the closed polygonal path $FGHF$ shown above, traversed in the **clockwise** direction. Express this integral as a sum of line integrals along $C_1, C_2$, and $C_3$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3704/Source/Images/4c07015f012adfb9c576718541a44fe8.png)" 13194139535230 false] [17592186179457 1145 "First, notice that we can represent $C$, which is traversed in clockwise, as the union of $3$ paths:\n\n$$\nC = \\{-C_1\\} \\cup \\{-C_2\\} \\cup C_3\n$$\n\nwhere $-C_1$ and $-C_2$ are the same paths as $C_1$ and $C_2$ but traversed in the opposite direction.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)F \\cdot dr &= ∫_(-C_{1})F \\cdot dr + ∫_(-C_{2})F \\cdot dr + ∫_(C_{3})F \\cdot dr \\\\\n&= - ∫_(C_{1})F \\cdot dr - ∫_(C_{2})F \\cdot dr + ∫_(C_{3})F \\cdot dr\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535230 false] [17592186179457 1145 "First, notice that we can represent $C,$ which is traversed in clockwise, as the union of $3$ paths:\n\n$$\nC = \\{-C_1\\} \\cup \\{-C_2\\} \\cup C_3,\n$$\n\nwhere $-C_1$ and $-C_2$ are the same paths as $C_1$ and $C_2$ but traversed in the opposite direction.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535230 true] [17592186179530 1100 "Let $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = (3x+2) \\: \\mathbf{i} - y^3 \\: \\mathbf{j}$ and let $C$ be a path in $\\mathbb R^2$. Write $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}$ in the form $\\displaystyle\\int_a^b P(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x + Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm d y$." 13194139535230 false] [17592186179530 1100 "Let $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = (3x+2) \\: \\mathbf{i} - y^3 \\: \\mathbf{j}$ and let $C$ be a path in $\\mathbb R^2.$ Write $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}$ in the form $\\displaystyle\\int_C P(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x +  Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm d y.$" 13194139535230 true] [17592186179530 1145 "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = P(x,y) \\: \\mathbf{i} + Q(x,y) \\: \\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\ndefined on $C$, we have\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\int\\limits_C P(x,y) \\: \\text{d}x + Q(x,y) \\: \\text{d}y\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nP = 3x+2\n$$\n\nand $Q = -y^3$. Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)F \\cdot dr &= ∫_(C)(3x + 2)dx - y^{3}dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535230 false] [17592186179530 1145 "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = P(x,y) \\: \\mathbf{i} + Q(x,y) \\: \\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\ndefined on $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\int\\limits_C P(x,y) \\: \\text{d}x + Q(x,y) \\: \\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $P = 3x+2$ and $Q = -y^3.$ Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}(3x+2)\\,\\text{d}x-{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535230 true] [17592186179599 1020 #edb/ref 17592186338855 13194139535230 true] [17592186179599 1020 #edb/ref 17592186338896 13194139535230 true] [17592186179599 1020 #edb/ref 17592186338903 13194139535230 true] [17592186179602 1020 #edb/ref 17592186338868 13194139535230 true] [17592186179602 1020 #edb/ref 17592186338875 13194139535230 true] [17592186179602 1020 #edb/ref 17592186338882 13194139535230 true] [17592186179602 1020 #edb/ref 17592186338889 13194139535230 true] [17592186179668 1020 #edb/ref 17592186338827 13194139535230 true] [17592186179668 1020 #edb/ref 17592186338834 13194139535230 true] [17592186179668 1020 #edb/ref 17592186338841 13194139535230 true] [17592186179668 1020 #edb/ref 17592186338848 13194139535230 true] [17592186179949 1017 #edb/ref 17592186177694 13194139535230 false] [17592186179949 1017 #edb/ref 17592186338821 13194139535230 true] [17592186179949 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535230 true] [17592186179949 1100 "Let $F(x, y, z) = y^{2}i - x^{2}j + 5k$ and let $C$ be a path in $R^{3}$. Which of the following is equivalent to $∫_(C)F \\cdot dr$?" 13194139535230 false] [17592186179949 1100 "Let $F(x,y,z)={y}^{2}\\,i-{x}^{2}\\,j+5\\,k$ and let $C$ be a path in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r?$" 13194139535230 true] [17592186179949 1145 "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field \n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=P(x,y,z)\\,i+Q(x,y,z)\\,j+R(x,y,z)\\,k\n$$\n\ndefined on $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{C}P(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y+R(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y+5\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535230 true] [17592186180109 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535230 true] [17592186180109 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060128/assets/0e4a78829ce8d1135d46dda1db59c968c0306937d9c5a45b24fa41648a679ea4.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a line segment from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $A(2,0),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(2,0)$ to the point $B(1,1).$\n\nGiven that ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2,$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=(x+y)\\,i+(x-y)\\,j,$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" 13194139535230 true] [17592186180109 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3704/Source/Images/q-39342.png)\n\nThe path $C_{1}$ consists of a line segment from the point $O(0, 0)$ to the point $A(2, 0)$, and the path $C_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(2, 0)$ to the point $B(1, 1)$.\n\nGiven that $∫_(C_{1})F \\cdot dr = 2$, evaluate $∫_(C)F \\cdot dr$, where $F(x, y) = (x + y)i + (x - y)j$, and $C = C_{1}∪C_{2}$." 13194139535230 false] [17592186180109 1145 "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the first integral, so we only need to work out the second.\n\nThe path ${C}_{2}$ is the line segment from $A$ to $B.$ It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{2}(t) & =a+t(b-a) \\\\  & =⟨2,0⟩+t(⟨1,1⟩-⟨2,0⟩) \\\\  & =⟨2-t,t⟩ \\\\  & =(2-t)\\,i+t\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0<t<1.$ Along this segment, we have $x=2-t$ and $y=t.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{2}(t)) & =(x+y)\\,i+(x-y)\\,j \\\\  & =(2-t+t)\\,i+(2-t-t)\\,j \\\\  & =2\\,i+(2-2t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{2}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =-i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{2}(t))\\cdot {r}_{2}^{′}(t) & =(2\\,i+(2-2t)\\,j)\\cdot (-i+j) \\\\  & =-2+(2-2t) \\\\  & =-2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =2+{\\int }_{0}^{1}F({r}_{2}(t))\\cdot {r}_{2}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2+{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2-{t}^{2}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2-(1-0) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535230 true] [17592186180111 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535230 true] [17592186180111 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060128/assets/512905447203d1d3a96781d20d586f9c4cf5cc9392fab50607b6fc77e9b00121.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a section of the parabola $y={x}^{2}$ from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $A(2,4),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(2,4)$ to the point $B(0,4).$\n\nGiven that ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-2,$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=x\\,i+y\\,j$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" 13194139535230 true] [17592186180111 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3704/Source/Images/q-39674.png)\n\nThe path $C_{1}$ consists of a section of the parabola $y = x^{2}$ from the point $O(0, 0)$ to the point $A(2, 4)$, and the path $C_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(2, 4)$ to the point $B(0, 4)$.\n\nGiven that $∫_(C_{2})F \\cdot dr =-2$, evaluate $∫_(C)F \\cdot dr$, where $F(x, y) = xi + yj$ and $C = C_{1}∪C_{2}$." 13194139535230 false] [17592186180111 1145 "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the second integral, so we only need to work out the first.\n\n${C}_{1}$ is the section of parabola $y={x}^{2}$ from $O$ to $A.$ It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1}(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0<t<2.$ Along this curve, we have $x=t$ and $y={t}^{2}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{1}(t)) & =x\\,i+y\\,j \\\\  & =t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})j \\\\  & =i+2t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{1}(t))\\cdot {r}_{1}^{′}(t) & =(t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j)\\cdot (i+2t\\,j) \\\\  & =t+2{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}F({r}_{1}(t))\\cdot {r}_{1}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t-2 \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(t+2{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t-2 \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{t}^{4}}{2}]}_{0}^{2}-2 \\\\  & =[2+8]-2 \\\\  & =10-2 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535230 true] [17592186180214 1017 #edb/ref 17592186179159 13194139535230 false] [17592186180214 1017 #edb/ref 17592186338862 13194139535230 true] [17592186180214 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535230 true] [17592186180214 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060128/assets/103c586778cdd8181fdc8396a92472df0525fc8e1c77fde9a7c3f731dca6df07.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the closed polygonal path $OABO,$ shown in the diagram above, traversed in the counterclockwise direction?" 13194139535230 true] [17592186180214 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3704/Source/Images/q-76473.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral $∫_(C)F \\cdot dr$, where $C$ is the closed polygonal path $OABO$, shown in the diagram above, traversed in the counterclockwise direction?" 13194139535230 false] [17592186180214 1145 "First, notice that we can represent $C,$ which is traversed in counterclockwise, as the union of $3$ paths:\n\n$$\nC={C}_{1}\\cup {-{C}_{2}}\\cup {C}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $-{C}_{2}$ is the same path as ${C}_{2}$ but traversed in the opposite direction.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535230 true] [17592186338821 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338822 13194139535230 true] [17592186338821 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338823 13194139535230 true] [17592186338821 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338824 13194139535230 true] [17592186338821 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338825 13194139535230 true] [17592186338821 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338826 13194139535230 true] [17592186338821 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535230 true] [17592186338821 1041 "selection" 13194139535230 true] [17592186338821 1104 #edb/ref 17592186338826 13194139535230 true] [17592186338821 1110 #uuid "ffa5c498-2566-5a14-bbf7-d5603ec0574e" 13194139535230 true] [17592186338822 1034 "{\\int }_{C}({y}^{2}+{x}^{2}+5)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z" 13194139535230 true] [17592186338822 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535230 true] [17592186338822 1111 #uuid "bf979321-309f-5a72-b8f1-6b36a93d2b79" 13194139535230 true] [17592186338823 1034 "{\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y" 13194139535230 true] [17592186338823 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535230 true] [17592186338823 1111 #uuid "668691db-ecd9-5882-